# Summary of changes for run 4f9a829c-1dbc-4cac-a257-d95aacf460d6
I wrote and published all five deliverables. `demo.py` runs cleanly. I couldn't compile `RESEARCH_PAPER.tex` or run the three plotting scripts here, because `pdflatex`, `numpy` and `matplotlib` aren't installed; I only checked them statically. I didn't rebuild the existing proof file either, since this phase was packaging only.

**`ARTICLE.md`** (about 2,300 words) is the popular-science piece. It explains:
- the "catch every \(i \le n\) with differences \(s-t\)" game and the Umans–Wang conjecture;
- the four-step counting proof of the corrected wall \(\alpha \ge 1-2\beta\), and why the earlier "\(\alpha+\beta \le 1\)" reading was wrong;
- zero slack at \((\tfrac13,\tfrac13)\), and the trivial block cover, which only reaches \(\alpha = 1-\beta\);
- why the wall is nearly reached by lopsided covers but not balanced ones;
- the exact optimum \(\mathrm{lcm}(1,\dots,n)+1\) for one-element sets;
- why "a 2-term progression hits every prime" proves nothing (Lemmas 6.2 and 6.3);
- why random sets fail.

**`RESEARCH_PAPER.md`** (about 3,000 words) and **`RESEARCH_PAPER.tex`** (an `amsart` version of the same paper) each contain an abstract, definitions, and full statements with proof sketches. The results covered are:
- the lcm wall \(\mathrm{lcm}(1,\dots,n) \le M^{|S||T|}\);
- the binomial wall \(4^{\lfloor n/2\rfloor} \le (n+1)M^{|S||T|}\);
- the logarithmic wall and \(\alpha \ge 1-2\beta\), with zero slack at \((\tfrac13,\tfrac13)\);
- the block cover and the exact one-element optimum;
- the Chinese-Remainder progression theorem.

They also include a computations section, a discussion and future directions. None of the three prose documents mentions proof tooling, file paths or internal names.

**`demo.py`** uses only the standard library. Its output gives:
- wall checks on explicit covers;
- optimal two-element covers for \(n \le 10\): the optimum stays within a factor 1.67–2.96 of \(\mathrm{lcm}(1,\dots,n)^{1/4}\);
- the trivial cover's exponent tending to \(2/3\);
- the Chinese-Remainder progressions;
- a random \((\tfrac13,\tfrac13)\) experiment that never covers fully (best 27–39% of the numbers up to \(n\)).

**`PACKAGE.json`** fills every field in the schema. It contains:
- 4 algorithms and 3 plotting scripts;
- 2 interactive HTML widgets: a playground for checking any \(S, T\) against the walls, and a draggable \((\alpha,\beta)\) diagram;
- a guided-tour page layout with collapsible proofs;
- the Phase A future directions, with two light edits;
- the full proof source, `Catalog/Tropical/UMWDivisorWall.lean`.

